    "narrative": "The evidence did not separate staffing level from order volume as the stronger driver of on-time rate. The run ended inconclusive, so neither explanation was established as the better one.\n\nBoth tested claims were weakened rather than supported: the claim that on-time rate is more strongly associated with staffing level than with order volume was weakened, and the reverse claim was also weakened. The run gathered evidence in both directions, but not enough to let either claim stand.\n\nIt tested the relationship with a correlation analysis, then a simple regression, then another correlation analysis. It stopped because there was insufficient evidence, and three questions remained open: which factor has the stronger association, whether the pattern is consistent across the observed data or driven by a few outliers, and which of the two competing claims, if either, actually holds.",
    "statement": "Mixed evidence for: On-time rate is more strongly associated with staffing level than with order volume.; Order volume is more strongly associated with on-time rate than staffing level is.",
